================================================================================
SCOPE
================================================================================

The license above covers the original Himalaya Non-stationary BEM code authored
by Durga Acharya: the setup, forward-model, inversion, plotting, and helper
scripts written for this project.

It does not extend to the third-party software and data redistributed with this
repository, which remain under the licenses and copyrights listed below, nor to
the model outputs and datasets that carry their own terms. Where the two differ,
the third-party terms govern those files.


================================================================================
ATTRIBUTION AND CITATION
================================================================================

The conditions in the BSD license above are the binding requirements for
redistribution: retain the copyright notice, the conditions, and the disclaimer.

Academic citation is a separate matter. It is a scholarly convention rather than
a condition of this license, and nothing here obliges anyone to cite a
particular work. That said, this software is research code, and its authors and
the authors of the components it builds on are credited in the normal way, by
citing the accompanying paper and the archived release. The "Citation" section
of README.md lists the references for the methods, codes, and data behind the
model, so that anyone building on it can credit the appropriate sources.


================================================================================
THIRD-PARTY SOFTWARE
================================================================================

  * hmmvp0.16/
    HMMVP: Software to Compute Hierarchical-Matrix Matrix-Vector Products,
    version 0.16, by Andrew M. Bradley. Licensed under the Eclipse Public
    License 1.0 (http://www.opensource.org/licenses/eclipse-1.0).
    See hmmvp0.16/readme.txt. Its author asks that work using the code cite:

        A. M. Bradley (2012). HMMVP: Software to Compute Matrix-Vector Products
        with an H-Matrix.

  * tools/CalcTriDisps.m, tools/tridisloc3d.m
    Triangular dislocation element routines, Copyright (c) 2006 Brendan Meade,
    released under the MIT license; the full notice is retained in each file
    header. tools/CalcTriDisps_O.m and tools/CalcTriStrains_O.m are modified
    versions of that code by Wen-Jeng Huang. These routines compute the stress
    kernel and the elastic Green's functions used by this software. The author
    asks that the code and the accompanying paper be cited as:

        Meade, B. J. (2007). Algorithms for the calculation of exact
        displacements, strains, and stresses for triangular dislocation elements
        in a uniform elastic half space. Computers & Geosciences, 33(8),
        1064-1075. doi:10.1016/j.cageo.2006.12.003

  * tools/Nikkhoo/, tools/TDstressHS.m
    Triangular dislocation routines, Copyright (c) 2014 Mehdi Nikkhoo
    (Nikkhoo & Walter, 2015), released under the MIT license; the full notice is
    retained in each file header. Bundled as an optional alternative kernel;
    every call site is commented out, so the default code path does not use it.

  * build_mesh/make_mesh_elastic.m, build_mesh/make_mesh_visco.m,
    tools/make_tri_mesh.m
    Triangular mesh generation based on code by Yo Fukushima (2010).


================================================================================
THIRD-PARTY DATA
================================================================================

  * ne_10m_coastline/
    Natural Earth 1:10m Coastline shapefile, version 4.1.0. Natural Earth raster
    and vector map data is in the public domain
    (https://www.naturalearthdata.com/about/terms-of-use/).

  * vel_field_lindsey/
    GPS velocities and derived baseline data from Lindsey et al. (2018).

  * build_mesh/him_contours_45.txt, build_mesh/*_rake_rate.txt
    Fault geometry derived from Slab2 (Hayes et al., 2018) and Hubbard et al.
    (2016); convergence rakes and rates derived from the Euler poles of
    Lindsey et al. (2018) and Panda & Lindsey (2024).

  * Green_function_visco/, visco_mesh/
    Interseismic Green's functions computed with VISCO3D (Pollitz), following
    the setup of Sherrill et al. (2026).

  * tools/cmap.mat
    Bundled colormap data.

Full references for all of the above are given in README.md, under
"Third-party software and data" and "Citation".
